+# QA report: newsletter signup shows success for rejected addresses
+
+## Summary
+
+The signup page tells the user **"You're subscribed. Check your inbox for the first issue."** even when the server **rejected** the address with `422`. Those people are never added to the subscriber list, so they never get an email.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`public/app.js` never looks at the HTTP response:
+
+```js
+await fetch('/api/subscribe', { ... });          // response discarded
+status.textContent = "You're subscribed. ...";   // shown unconditionally
+form.reset();
+```
+
+`fetch` only rejects on network failure, not on 4xx/5xx. So any server-side rejection lands on the success branch. The form is also reset, which clears the address the user would need to correct.
+
+## Which addresses are affected
+
+The browser's `<input type="email">` check is looser than the server's `validate()` in `server.js`. Any address that passes the browser check but fails the server check gets the false success message. Tested in Chromium via Playwright:
+
+| Address entered          | Server response                                       | Page shows          |
+|--------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|---------------------|
+| `jane@example.com`       | 201 `{"ok":true}`                                     | You're subscribed ✅ |
+| `jane+news@example.com`  | 422 `Addresses with + are not supported.`             | You're subscribed ❌ |
+| `jane@localhost`         | 422 `Enter a valid email address.`                    | You're subscribed ❌ |
+| `jane@example.c`         | 422 `Enter a valid email address.`                    | You're subscribed ❌ |
+| `jane@example.123`       | 422 `Enter a valid email address.`                    | You're subscribed ❌ |
+
+**Plus-addressing (`name+tag@domain`) is by far the most likely real-world trigger.** Gmail and Fastmail users commonly use it for newsletters. The other cases are mostly typos.
+
+The same false success would also appear for any `5xx` from the API, because the code never checks the status.
+
+## Reproduce
+
+1. `node server.js`
+2. Open http://localhost:3000, enter `jane+news@example.com`, click **Subscribe**.
+3. The page says "You're subscribed". DevTools → Network shows `POST /api/subscribe` → `422 {"error":"Addresses with + are not supported."}`.
+
+## Automated test
+
+`e2e/subscribe.test.js` (node:test + Playwright, starts `server.js` itself on a free port):
+
+```
+node --test e2e/subscribe.test.js
+```
+
+- **Happy path** (`jane@example.com` → 201 → success message): passes.
+- **Three rejected addresses** (`+` address, `@localhost`, 1-letter TLD): for each, the test checks that the server returns 422, that the page does **not** say "You're subscribed", that the server's error text is shown, and that the input keeps the address. **All three currently fail.**
+
+Current result: 1 pass, 3 fail. To confirm the test catches this bug and nothing else, I applied a minimal `res.ok` check to a **scratch copy** of `app.js`. With that change all 4 tests passed. The repo's app code was not changed.
+
+## Suggested fix (not applied)
+
+- In `app.js`, check `res.ok`. On failure, show the server's `error` message and don't reset the form.
+- Product decision: decide whether rejecting `+` addresses is intended. They are valid email addresses, and rejecting them blocks a common group of subscribers. If the rule stays, it should at least be shown to the user, which the fix above does.
+
+## Other observations (outside this bug)
+
+- `server.js` never sends any email. On success it only adds the address to an in-memory `Set`. The "check your inbox" promise depends on some other process that isn't in this repo. Because the `Set` is in memory, every subscriber is lost when the server restarts. If there is no external mailer reading from somewhere, even valid signups won't get an email. Worth checking with whoever owns delivery.
+- Duplicate signups return 201 again (fine), and addresses are lowercased before storing.
